{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 18, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 as the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early life was marked by political instability in the Crusader states. Raymond inherited the county at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated. Due to his youth a regency was established with his mother Hodierna playing a key role in governance. During this time the County of Tripoli faced threats from Muslim forces and internal power struggles. Raymond was eventually declared of age in 1156 and began to rule independently. His reign coincided with the rise of Nur ad-Din and later Saladin who both sought to unite Muslim territories against the Crusaders.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III became one of the most influential nobles in the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was appointed regent of Jerusalem in 1174 after the death of King Baldwin III and the minority of Baldwin IV. As regent he managed the affairs of the kingdom during a time of great vulnerability. His leadership was challenged by the rising power of the Knights Templar and the ambitious noble Guy of Lusignan. Raymond often clashed with Guy over strategy and authority. He advocated for diplomacy with Muslim leaders and was willing to negotiate truces to preserve Christian holdings. This approach put him at odds with more militant factions within the kingdom. Despite his efforts Raymond was eventually removed from the regency in 1177 due to political maneuvering by his rivals.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III played a crucial role in the events leading to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was a member of the council that advised King Guy of Lusignan to march against Saladin. Raymond warned against the move but was overruled. After the disastrous defeat at Hattin Raymond was captured and held prisoner by Saladin. He was released after a ransom was paid. He returned to Tripoli and spent his final years trying to rebuild his shattered county. Raymond died in 1187 shortly after his release. His death marked the end of a long and turbulent era in the history of the Crusader states. He was remembered as a cautious and strategic leader who sought peace in a time of relentless war. His legacy is one of resilience and diplomacy amid overwhelming odds.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008450583554804325}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014097276143729687}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.09045735746622086}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0609570381348021e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009419057168997824}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00014351768186315894}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -2.455681169521995e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.4740951657295227}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0027385372668504715}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -3.3378546504536644e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.2993727978027891e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -2.0265558760002023e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.